 In their ransom notes, Gunra actors typically demand that victims initiate negotiation discussions within five to seven days via a Tor-based negotiation portal or qTox, or risk having their data leaked on Gunra’s DLS. The FBI observed Gunra actors attempting to communicate directly with management staff at victim companies via email to solicit ransom payments with limited success. Gunra actors instructed victims to send ransom payments to specific cryptocurrency wallet addresses and generally started negotiations at arbitrarily high ransom amounts (over tens of millions in US dollars).
 
-If Gunra victims do not negotiate or pay ransom, the actors publicly disclose the victims on their DLS and offer a preview of victims’ leaked data. This preview typically includes a directory listing of a victim’s exposed OneDrive and SharePoint files, but not the content of the files. Between June and July of 2025, Gunra actors operated a clearnet mirror of their Tor-based DLS at domain datapub.news. By March 2026, Gunra had moved their original Tor-based DLS to a different .onion address. On Gunra’s current Tor-based DLS, the actors advertise the sale of datasets from specific victims and instruct interested parties to contact them via qTox for more information.
+If Gunra victims do not negotiate or pay ransom, the actors publicly disclose the victims on their DLS and offer a preview of victims’ leaked data. This preview typically includes a directory listing of a victim’s exposed OneDrive and SharePoint files, but not the content of the files. Between June and July of 2025, Gunra actors operated a clearnet mirror of their Tor-based DLS at domain ykxthqpddj.invalid. By March 2026, Gunra had moved their original Tor-based DLS to a different .onion address. On Gunra’s current Tor-based DLS, the actors advertise the sale of datasets from specific victims and instruct interested parties to contact them via qTox for more information.
 
